  "name": "Question 20",
  "problem": "A particle moves with a constant acceleration of $(i-2j)\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.$ The velocity of the particle is $(2i-5j)\\,\\text{m/s}$ when $t=0\\,\\text{s}.$ Find the time when the velocity of the particle equals $(3i-j)\\,\\text{m/s}.$",
  "worked_solution": "Recall that when a particle moves with constant acceleration, the velocity vector at time $t$ can be found using\n\n$$\nv=u+at,\n$$\n\nwhere $u$ is the initial velocity, and $a$ is the acceleration of the particle.\n\nSubstituting \n\n$$\na=i-2j,\\,u=2i-5j,\\,v=3i-j\n$$\n\ninto the equation above, we can attempt to solve for $t,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =u+at \\\\ 3i-j & =(2i-5j)+(i-2j)t \\\\ i+4j & =(i-2j)t \\\\ i+4j & =ti-2tj\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the $i$ and $j$ components in the last equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}1=t \\\\ 4=-2t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich has no solution.",
